From: MacFinals
Subject: [PATCH] winebus.sys: Xbox 360 bus for any XInput pad, on macOS 15 and later too

Pads in XInput mode that are not Microsoft's (8BitDo's "normal" mode on its dongle and cable,
and many others) present a USB interface of class 255/93/1, which is not HID: macOS, its
GameController framework and SDL never see them, so games got no controller. This bus could
read them, but it accepted only Microsoft's ids and switched itself off on macOS 15+.

- Matches any device with that interface; on macOS 15+ leaves Microsoft's own wired 360 pads
  to GameController.framework (they reach Wine through SDL), so nothing is listed twice.
- Opens only that interface, found in the registry, instead of opening the device and setting
  its configuration (composite pads have other interfaces in use by macOS).
- Looks up the interrupt pipes instead of assuming the IN pipe is 1.
- Sends what XUSB/xpad send first: the vendor control read 0xC1 0x01 0x0100 and the
  player-1 LED command.
- Forwards only input reports (00 14 ...); LED/connection/headset messages reached the game
  as input. A read error ends the read loop instead of re-queuing forever.
- Keeps the sticks' Y as the pad sends it (up = positive): winebus gamepad reports use the
  XUSB convention since Wine 9 (bus_sdl.c flips SDL's Y to match); the old inversion here
  inverted both sticks' up/down in games.
- Says when the interface is held exclusively (macOS Steam does that to wired pads).

Measured with tests/controller_latency/xinput_usb_probe.c (same IOUSBLib path): the 8BitDo
Ultimate 2 on its dongle reports at 1000 Hz (1.00 ms median), against 25 Hz in Nintendo mode.
